WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:05.100
Now the units, and one of them is the most useful fact in all of chart work.

2
00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:09.075
One minute of latitude equals one nautical mile.

3
00:00:09.325 --> 00:00:12.025
About six thousand seventy-six feet.

4
00:00:12.275 --> 00:00:14.875
So the side of your chart is a ruler.

5
00:00:15.125 --> 00:00:20.600
A statute mile, the one on highway signs, is five thousand two hundred eighty feet.

6
00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:24.675
So a nautical mile is about fifteen percent longer.

7
00:00:24.925 --> 00:00:27.850
A knot is one nautical mile per hour.

8
00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:29.950
It’s already a speed.

9
00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:32.825
So what’s wrong with saying knots per hour?

10
00:00:33.825 --> 00:00:36.600
You’d be saying miles per hour per hour.

11
00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:38.575
Just say knots.

12
00:00:38.825 --> 00:00:40.500
And one warning.

13
00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:44.725
A minute of longitude is only a mile long at the equator.

14
00:00:44.975 --> 00:00:47.525
It shrinks as you go toward the poles.

15
00:00:47.775 --> 00:00:51.475
So never measure distance on the top or bottom scale.

16
00:00:51.725 --> 00:00:55.325
Distance comes from the side, and only from the side.
